      en: "The ruins of the German outpost in Makamba date from around 1900, when the area was part of German East Africa. The outpost served as a military station and administrative center to control the local population. Remains include stone foundation walls, a collapsed guardhouse, and a well. After Germany's defeat in WWI, Belgian troops took over the station but abandoned it in the 1920s. Today, the ruins are overgrown with vegetation and stand as a testament to early colonial history. The remote site is accessible only on foot and attracts history enthusiasts and hikers.",
