      en: "The royal compound of Bubanza was a traditional power center of Burundi's monarchy. It served as the residence of the king (Mwami) and the administrative seat of the kingdom. The compound consisted of several buildings, including the royal palace, assembly halls, and quarters for courtiers and servants. After the monarchy was abolished in 1966, the compound fell into disrepair. Today, the remains are a historical site recalling pre-colonial governance structures. The compound holds cultural significance and is used by local communities for ceremonies. Traditional architecture with thatched roofs and mud walls is typical of the region.",
